Refresh already-stored Lunchflow transactions instead of freezing them.

LunchflowItem::Importer filtered the incoming payload down to ids it had never
seen, stating the intent outright: "we treat them as immutable snapshots". First
write wins forever — every later correction the bank makes to amount, date or
isPending is discarded. Because the entry is deep-merged from this stored payload
on every sync, the stale values are then re-applied indefinitely, so an entry can
never self-heal and a wrong pending flag stays wrong forever.

That is not a cosmetic problem. A stale pending row is the candidate set for
Account::ProviderImportAdapter#find_pending_transaction, whose claim is
destructive; observed in production, a payload held 196 pending rows (oldest
dated 2020) while the provider reported 12, and an amount frozen mid-authorisation
at -39.96 against -41.17 actually charged.

Lunchflow transaction ids are stable across the pending→posted transition —
verified against a live provider, where 159 transactions stored while pending are
now returned settled under the same id, with zero id changes. So an id already
held identifies the same transaction and can be refreshed in place.

Merges rather than replaces, so keys the provider stops returning are preserved.
Rows aged out of the provider's response window are untouched, since only ids
present in the incoming payload are refreshed. Blank-id rows are keyed by content
hash and keep the existing dedupe path. User edits remain protected downstream by
protected_from_sync?.

